"Inside the oven is a part called a magnetron. It makes microwaves, which are invisible waves like the ones that carry radio and Wi-Fi signals. The cooking space is a metal box, and microwaves bounce off metal, so they fly back and forth inside. Even the window in the door has a metal mesh in it. The holes are much smaller than the waves, so light gets out but the microwaves stay in.": "7d585634d0e7",
"Light and radio signals travel as waves.": "2413b57cee46",
"Metal is a different story. Microwaves cannot pass through metal. At sharp points, like the tips of a fork or the edges of crumpled foil, electric charge piles up and jumps through the air as sparks. So never put metal in a microwave oven. Eggs in their shells and tightly sealed containers are dangerous too, because steam builds up inside until they burst.": "6d2595a6b145",
"Now for the trick. A water molecule has a slightly positive end and a slightly negative end. The microwaves push and pull on these ends, first one way and then the other, about 2.45 billion times a second. The water molecules try to follow, flipping back and forth. They bump into the molecules around them, and all that jostling is heat. The food heats itself.": "1ebd150d5ad7",
"Put a bowl of cold soup in the microwave oven and press start. One minute later the soup is steaming. But touch the rim of the bowl: it is hardly warm. A stove heats the pot first, and the pot heats the soup. A microwave oven seems to skip the pot. How does it do that?": "0f85fb8f49e6",
"Put a bowl of cold soup in the microwave oven and press start. One minute later the soup is steaming. But touch the rim of the bowl: it is hardly warm. A stove heats the pot first, and the pot heats the soup. A microwave oven seems to skip the pot. How does it do that? Inside the oven is a part called a magnetron. It makes microwaves, which are invisible waves like the ones that carry radio and Wi-Fi signals. The cooking space is a metal box, and microwaves bounce off metal, so they fly back and forth inside. Even the window in the door has a metal mesh in it. The holes are much smaller than the waves, so light gets out but the microwaves stay in. Now for the trick. A water molecule has a slightly positive end and a slightly negative end. The microwaves push and pull on these ends, first one way and then the other, about 2.45 billion times a second. The water molecules try to follow, flipping back and forth. They bump into the molecules around them, and all that jostling is heat. The food heats itself. That explains the cool bowl. A dry plate or bowl has almost no water in it, so the waves pass right through. It also explains why ice heats slowly: in ice, the water molecules are locked in place and can hardly turn. And because the bouncing waves are strong in some places and weak in others, the oven has hot spots and cold spots. The turntable carries the food through both. Metal is a different story. Microwaves cannot pass through metal. At sharp points, like the tips of a fork or the edges of crumpled foil, electric charge piles up and jumps through the air as sparks. So never put metal in a microwave oven. Eggs in their shells and tightly sealed containers are dangerous too, because steam builds up inside until they burst. The idea was found by accident. In 1945 an American engineer, Percy Spencer, was working with radar when he noticed that a candy bar in his pocket had melted. The first thing he cooked on purpose was popcorn. Are microwaves dangerous to eat? No. They do not make food radioactive, and the moment the oven stops, the waves are gone. Only the heat is left.": "61fb97bd1390",
